            <lineage>
                <LI_Lineage>
                    <statement>
                        <gco:CharacterString>ACQUISITION: Airborne Laser Scanning (ALS) data was acquired from a fixed wing aircraft between 2019-06-11 and 2019-08-16</gco:CharacterString>
                    </statement>
                    <processStep>
                        <LI_ProcessStep>
                            <description>
                                <gco:CharacterString>GPS Processing&#13;
For the most part, network solutions using the SmartnetAus network were used, with single base as a backup. In each case, the option producing the best results was used and integrated into the data.&#13;
&#13;
Raw data processing&#13;
Raw data processing using RiProcess occurred without incident, although MTA issues were located and handled accordingly.&#13;
&#13;
Strip Alignment&#13;
Strip alignment was conducted in Bayesmap StripAlign using a two-step approach in which flights were first processed against their own cross ties to allow for daily system calibration before processed calibrated flight lines were reprocessed against dedicated cross ties flown across the entire project area from a single flight. This created a homogenous block of calibrated adjusted LiDAR data. Processing residuals were within acceptable tolerances and no visible steps, tilts or shifts could be identified between flight lines in final processed data.&#13;
Flight line matching is an important process that resolves differences between LiDAR flight lines that are caused by small residual errors in GPS position or Inertial measurement solutions. A typical processed LiDAR project, with accurate GPS residuals and recent sensor calibration, will exhibit stepping or mis-joins between adjacent flight lines, especially between flight lines captured on different days or GPS solutions. &#13;
&#13;
Traditionally these differences are minimised by calculating and applying calibration correction in terms of Roll, Pitch and Heading and height corrections. These are applied to whole flights or individual flight lines. This process corrects major differences however can leave some unresolved isolated issues which may cause a visible step in derived gridded products and contours at these locations. Differences between strips and cross ties are no longer visible following calibration correction in the result shown below.&#13;
&#13;
The remaining differences between flight lines are the result of minute localised variations in GPS accuracy and IMU measurement residuals. A suitable drift correction can be applied to flight lines which results in homogeneously matched LiDAR swaths. &#13;
&#13;
Gridded products&#13;
DEM and DSM were generated using LAStools. DEM was generated from ground and model key points using a triangulated interpolation surface.&#13;
 &#13;
Hydro flattening was performed on the DEM using a semi-automated method of applying the minimum DEM height within data void regions from a 1m grid generated from all LiDAR returns excluding those classified as water.&#13;
 &#13;
DSM was generated from non-ground point classes as a highest point in cell grid. Where no-data values exist within the resulting grid the elevation of the DEM was adopted.&#13;
 &#13;
Contours were generated to engineering accuracy levels using a specialised ground filtering technique to ensure no bow ties or line crosses occurred.</gco:CharacterString>
